-- A grammar that says where a parse may carry on past an error.
--
-- `ERROR` is a terminal the grammar may name, numbered past the end of input.
-- Naming it gives each start symbol two more entry points, which put one on
-- the stack where a parse fails and carry on from there; the two every grammar
-- has still stop at the first error, exactly as they did.
--
-- The token type is one Puppy did not write, which is where the numbering has
-- a trap in it: classifying a token the grammar never declared has to land
-- outside the table, and the number one past the declared terminals is the one
-- `ERROR` has.

%{
import Puppy.Fixture.External.Token as T
%}

%tokentype { T.Token }

%token PLUS "+" { T.At _ T.Plus }
%token { Int } NUMBER "a number" { T.At _ (T.Number $$) }

-- A start symbol named after a helper the generated module writes, so that the
-- two are shown not to collide. `puppyFromRecovered` is what the helper is
-- called, and the `puppy` prefix is reserved for exactly this reason.
%start { String } total fromRecovered

%type { String } total fromRecovered expr

%%

total:
  | e = expr { e }

fromRecovered:
  | e = expr { e }

expr:
  | n = NUMBER                 { show n }
  | a = expr PLUS b = expr %shift { a <> "+" <> b }
  | e = ERROR                  { "?" <> show e.position }
